7. Contributions

By submitting a contribution, you grant the copyright holder a perpetual,
irrevocable, worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive license to use,
reproduce, modify, distribute, sublicense and relicense your contribution
under any terms, and you confirm you have the right to grant it. You keep
copyright in your contribution.

You also grant the copyright holder, and everyone who gets the Software
from the copyright holder, a perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide,
royalty-free license to make, use, sell, import and otherwise deal in your
contribution and the Software under any patent you can license that your
contribution, alone or combined with the Software it was made for, would
infringe.

You consent to the copyright holder changing, adapting or leaving out your
contribution, and you will not assert any moral right in it against the
copyright holder or anyone the copyright holder licenses. If someone
infringes the copyright in your contribution as part of the Software, you
will join or support, at the copyright holder's cost, any action the
copyright holder takes against them.

These rights do not lapse however long they go unused. You give them in
return for the copyright holder reviewing your contribution and for the
rights section 4b gives you.

Sign off every commit of a pull request (git commit -s) to confirm that you
agree to this section. Pull requests whose commits are not signed off may
be refused.

8. Changes to this license

Only the copyright holder may publish a new version of this license. A new
version counts only once the copyright holder has published it both in the
official repository and in a release of the official app. A change anyone
else makes to the license file, including through a pull request, is not a
new version.

A new version that only adds rights applies as soon as it is published. A
new version that takes rights away applies to you once you have been told
about it (for example in the official app or on the official website) and
have accepted it, or have kept using the Software for 30 days after being
told. It never makes earlier use a breach. If you do not accept a new
version, you must stop using the Software and delete every copy and build
of it that you hold, except as section 2 allows for app-store copies.

9. Termination

Every permission in this license is given on the conditions in sections 2
to 6; any use outside them is not licensed.

If you break any term of this license, every permission it gives you ends
at once and automatically, and getting another copy of the Software does
not give you a new license. You must then stop using the Software and
delete every copy and build of it, original or modified, that you hold,
except as section 2 allows for app-store copies. Sections 6, 7, 9, 10, 11,
12 and 13 still apply after termination. The copyright holder may restore
your rights by written permission.

10. Third-party components

Third-party code included in or downloaded by the Software keeps its own
license, and nothing in this license restricts what that license lets you
do with it. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md lists the components the official app
ships and their licenses.

These files of ours are under a different license, as their notices say:
lre/proot/ (GPL-2.0-or-later), except lre/proot/talloc/memset_explicit.c
(LGPL-3.0-or-later). Apart from third-party code, no other file is under a
different license, whatever notice it carries, unless this section lists
it; a notice added by anyone other than the copyright holder changes
nothing.

You may extract, change and share third-party components under their own
licenses, and doing so is never a breach of this license. For a component
we build with changes of our own, that covers the upstream code and the
changes its license requires us to offer under it, such as our changes to
PRoot and talloc. Our other changes stay under this license.

For each binary the official app ships that contains an LGPL library, you
may modify that binary for your own use, including by relinking it with a
changed version of the library, and reverse engineer it to debug those
changes. Sections 4 and 5 do not limit this.

The complete corresponding source of the GPL and LGPL components the
official app ships is published on the official repository's releases page,
or offered in writing in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md where it says so.

11. Your files and your work

What you make with the official app, such as your projects, code, text and
the output of its agents, is yours; this license claims no right in it. The
notes and settings the app writes into your own files (such as the block it
adds to an agent's instructions file) may stay there, and you may keep,
change and share those files as part of your work. This does not let you
share the app's programs, tools or other parts of the Software.

